            Debug.Assert((Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount)) || (!Old_arrListContainsX && !New_arrListContainsX && New_Ret == -1));
        }
    }
}


A: Test methods in PEX/PexRunner can be quite complex. In your particular example, you're testing the IndexOf method of ArrayList, but you've not mentioned the nature of the parameters.
PEX has a set of preconditions and postconditions that it applies to your method. While these can serve as a useful way to test your method, PEX also has a "search algorithm" - a binary search - to determine valid inputs for your tests.
For your test, you would ideally like to test:


*

*A list with no items

*A list with one item (should return 0 if that item is the one being searched for)

*A list with multiple items (include cases where element is not in the list, which should return -1)

*Variants with duplicate items in the list


If the list is empty, IndexOf should return -1. If the list contains 1 item and that item is what you searched for, it should return 0. If what you're searching for is not in the list, it should return -1.
This kind of coverage is typically enough for "smoke testing" an ArrayList-like class, and should be sufficient to find simple failures/edge cases. If you need more rigorous testing, you can do more detailed testing.
Remember that the "smoke test" (covering edge cases) and the "more detailed test" might have different precondition. For example, your arraylist is empty in the smoke test and is not empty in the detailed test.
If you are having trouble finding edge cases using PEX, PexRunner can take an input list and find values that are outside the range of your search.
You might wanna check out PexSettings.InputGenerationSettings.MaxConstraintSolverTime and InputGenerationSettings.MaxItemsToConductSearch.
I hope this helps a bit. If you provide more detail on the nature of your parameters, I can give more specific advice.
EDIT: A list with duplicate items will be tested automatically by Pex, it should return smallest index if element is duplicate

