A: Although I agree with your approach for PexAssumeUnderTest, I want to suggest another approach, which may help some of the developers with some particular issue in their solution. Here it is:

You can use PexMembers to create different member functions and PexAllocation to generate different instances or arrange different states. 

[PexMethod]
[PexAllowedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
[PexExpectedExceptionWithMessage("*{ArrayList}*", "Cannot add duplicate elements to a list of type 'System.Collections.ArrayList'.")]
public void AddDuplicate(ArrayList list, int newEntry)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(list.IndexOf(newEntry) < 0);
    AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(list.Contains( newEntry));

    int preCount = list.Count;

    list.Add(newEntry);
    int postCount = list.Count;

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("before Add", preCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("after Add", postCount);

    Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(newEntry), "The list should contain the added item");
    Assert.AreEqual(preCount + 1, postCount, "The length of the list should have increased by 1 after the add");
}

In this contract test, it is assumed that an array list object passed in as input is not containing the integer that is about to be added (via PexAssumeUnderTest). The actual method being called is a PexMembers (in this case, Add), and it's assumed that the precondition holds before entering the method. The rest is a standard contract test.

Please note that the actual implementation of the function can significantly vary depending upon your project’s business logic. 

I hope this helps others save themselves from issues they can't easily reproduce in a simple scenario like this.

This is an updated version of your test:

The updated version of the PexMethod, with the added PexAllowedException and PexExpectedExceptionWithMessage attributes:

[PexMethod]
[PexAllowedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
[PexExpectedExceptionWithMessage("*{ArrayList}*", "Cannot add duplicate elements to a list of type 'System.Collections.ArrayList'.")]
public void AddDuplicate(ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.IndexOf(x) < 0);
    AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(x));
    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Add(x);
    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    // Test if we're not adding duplicates.
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount);
}

