Anyways in answer to your question. Here is a basic contract test for the ArrayList.IndexOf method.
public void IndexOfContract([PexAssumeValid] ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid] int val)
{
    int ret = arrList.IndexOf(val);
    if (arrList.Contains(val))
    {
        // if val is in the list, last index of val should be greater or equal to index of val
        Assert.IsTrue(ret <= arrList.LastIndexOf(val));
    }
    else
    {
        // if val is not in the list, index of val is -1
        Assert.AreEqual(-1, ret);
    }
}

This code assumes that arrList is a valid ArrayList object and val is a valid integer value to be tested in the arrList object.
It performs IndexOf method, then checks that if the value is in the list, the result should be less or equal to the last index of the value in the list. If the value is not in the list, the IndexOf should return -1.
Remember to always use PexAssumeValid to accept valid inputs to your testing methods, because the input validation happens at a different stage.
I hope this gets you on the right track.
This is a basic example. Depending on your specific use-case, you might need a more detailed testing scenario. Please provide further context if you need more help.
Edit: The contract test I included above is an example and might not work for your exact test or implementation. It would be useful to see your exact test or your ArrayList implementation for more precise guidance.

A: Here is a PEX contract test for the ArrayList.Remove() method, which also includes the effect of the removal on the Count property.
    /// <summary>
    /// Contract test for the ArrayList.Remove() method, including the effect on the Count property. 
    /// </summary>
    [PexMethod]
    public void RemoveContract([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrayList, [PexAssumeValid]int index)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrayList.Count && index >= 0);

        object oldObj = arrayList[index];
        int oldCount = arrayList.Count;

        arrayList.Remove(index);

        int newCount = arrayList.Count;
        bool containsRemovedElement = arrayList.Contains(oldObj);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Old Count", oldCount);   
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("New Count", newCount);   
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Removed Element", oldObj);

        Assert.AreEqual(oldCount - 1, newCount); // One less element should be in the list after the removal.
        Assert.IsFalse(containsRemovedElement);    // The removed element should not be in the list anymore.
    }

Here, we're assuming a valid ArrayList and a valid index, and performing Remove. We're then checking the Count property of our list after the Remove. We expect that the Count property has been decreased by 1, and that the element was no longer in our list afterwards.
This code has been written in C# .NET and utilizes Pex (PexLab) Fuzz Testing. This specific example is for the ArrayList.Remove() method. You may adjust the assertion or test conditions depending on your implementation and expected--------------------	    // that New_Ret == expected (if the item does not exist in the array, the method should return -1)
		bool PositiveTest = (Old_arrListContainsX) ?  (New_Ret>=0 && New_Ret<=Old_arrListLastIndexOfX): (New_Ret==-1); 
		Debug.Assert(PositiveTest);
			
		//an assertion used with "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that @@@ The list size has not changed.
		Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

		// an assertion used with "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that @@@ If X exists in old arr list, the index of X should in the range [0,old_arrListCount-1] otherwise -1.
		int Range = (Old_arrListContainsX) ? Old_arrListCount-1 : -1;
		Assert.AreEqual(Old_x, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
		Assert.AreEqual(Range, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

		//an assertion used with "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that @@@ If X exists in the new array then IndexOf(x) should be greater or equals old count.
		Assert.IsTrue( !Old_arrListContainsX || (New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount));

		//an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ if x exists in old list, New_Ret should be greater then Old_arrListLastIndexOfX otherwise it should be -1.
		Debug.Assert((Old_arrListContainsX) ? (New_Ret > Old_arrListLastIndexOfX): (New_Ret==-1));
        }
    }
}
```

